= Conclusions <sec:conclusions>

Interrupted creep testing at 923 K combined with atom-probe reconstruction on
the same gauge lengths gives the following.

+ The threshold stress of a 20Cr-25Ni-Nb austenitic steel rises with exposure,
  from 61 MPa in the solution-treated condition to 74 MPa after 3,000 h, while
  the Orowan stress of its MX dispersion falls from 58 MPa to 32 MPa over the
  same interval. The threshold and the dispersion are anticorrelated, so the
  dispersion is not the sole origin of the threshold.
+ Grain-boundary niobium enrichment rises from 2.1 at.% to 7.4 at.% over the
  same exposure, and the fitted threshold is linear in that enrichment with a
  slope of 2.0 MPa per at.% and a residual scatter of 2 MPa across two heats
  and five conditions.
+ A second heat with 14% less niobium and a statistically indistinguishable
  particle spacing creeps 3.4 times faster at 190 MPa, which is consistent with
  segregation control and not with dispersion control.
+ The McLean fit of @eq:mclean gives $Delta G_"seg" = -41$ kJ mol#super[-1] and
  an equilibrium boundary concentration of 11.2 at.% at 923 K, so laboratory
  exposures of a few thousand hours observe an alloy that is roughly two-thirds
  of the way to its segregated state. Component-relevant extrapolations should
  not assume the boundary chemistry is static.
+ The description is restricted to 150 MPa to 220 MPa at 923 K. Above that
  interval the fracture mode changes to transgranular and the threshold
  attributed to boundary chemistry no longer applies.

The practical implication is that niobium specification for this alloy class
should be set by the boundary enrichment it can sustain rather than by the
carbon it can tie up, and that accelerated testing which reaches rupture in
under 1,000 h will systematically understate the long-term strength of the
higher-niobium heats.

